      "id": "sql-10-3-md-6",
      "type": "overview",
      "heading": "A running balance per customer",
      "content": "A ledger screen shows a running total. Rebuild it with a window function (5.5) and compare it line by line:\n```sql\nSELECT c.full_name AS customer, l.ledger_id, l.entry_type, l.amount,\n       CASE l.entry_type WHEN 'payment' THEN -l.amount ELSE l.amount END AS signed_amount,\n       SUM(CASE l.entry_type WHEN 'payment' THEN -l.amount ELSE l.amount END)\n         OVER (PARTITION BY c.customer_id ORDER BY l.created_at, l.ledger_id) AS running_balance\n```\n| FROM   appointment_ledger l | JOIN   appointments a ON a.appointment_id = l.appointment_id |\n|---|---|\n| JOIN   customers    c ON c.customer_id    = a.customer_id | ORDER  BY c.customer_id, l.created_at, l.ledger_id; |\n\ncustomer      | ledger_id | entry_type | amount  | signed_amount | running_balance\n| Rajan Pandey  \\| 3         \\| charge     \\| 1200.00 \\| 1200.00       \\| 1200.00 | Rajan Pandey  \\| 4         \\| payment    \\| 1500.00 \\| -1500.00      \\| -300.00 |\n|---|---|\n| Sunita Basnet \\| 1         \\| charge     \\| 800.00  \\| 800.00        \\| 800.00 | Sunita Basnet \\| 5         \\| charge     \\| 800.00  \\| 800.00        \\| 1600.00 |\n\nRajan's balance is negative (a credit for a payment he shouldn't have needed to make), and Sunita is charged 1,600 for an 800 service. The sign convention (refunds increase what is owed) is an assumption, and the running balance at the last entry must equal the customer's outstanding-balance figure elsewhere in the app. Always use the unique ledger_id as a tiebreaker in the window's ORDER BY, or two entries in the same second give an unstable balance (5.5).\nSchedule and slot checks for the calendar screen\nThe provider's day view is a filtered, sorted list:\n```sql\nSELECT a.appointment_id, TIME(a.starts_at) AS starts, TIME(a.ends_at) AS ends,\n```\n| c.full_name AS customer, s.name AS service, a.status | FROM   appointments a | JOIN   customers c ON c.customer_id = a.customer_id |\n|---|---|---|\n| JOIN   services  s ON s.service_id  = a.service_id | WHERE  a.employee_id = 2 AND DATE(a.starts_at) = '2026-09-22' AND a.status IN ('booked','completed') | ORDER  BY a.starts_at, a.appointment_id; |\n\n| appointment_id \\| starts   \\| ends     \\| customer     \\| service      \\| status | 1              \\| 10:00:00 \\| 10:30:00 \\| Anita Sharma \\| Consultation \\| booked |\n|---|---|\n| 101            \\| 10:15:00 \\| 10:45:00 \\| Anita Sharma \\| Consultation \\| booked | 2              \\| 11:00:00 \\| 12:00:00 \\| Rajan Pandey \\| Full Checkup \\| booked |\n\nAnita appears in two overlapping bookings with the same provider, so a customer double-booking check (the APT-03 join keyed on customer_id) would fire here too. On big tables, replace DATE(a.starts_at) = '...' with a range on starts_at so an index can be used (2.1). To test slot availability, the expected set of free slots is the working hours minus the union of booked intervals, and the boundary cases are the same list you'd test for leave: back-to-back bookings, one minute of overlap, a booking that spans the lunch break, and one that ends after closing.",
